Memory - how much the computer has, and where it goes
=====================================================

This desktop is a Risc PC with a StrongARM processor and 256MB of RAM
(two 128MB memory modules, the most a Risc PC can hold), 2MB of video
RAM (VRAM) for the screen and 4MB of ROM. You can make it a smaller
machine, down to a 4MB A7000, and share its memory out as on RISC OS.

Contents:

  1) Seeing the memory: the Task Manager
  2) Changing the amount of RAM
  3) Sharing the memory out
  4) Limits
  5) Things to know


1) Seeing the memory: the Task Manager
--------------------------------------

Click SELECT on the Acorn icon at the right of the icon bar. The Tasks
window shows:

  Application tasks     each program and the memory it has (its
                        "slot"); Next, the slot the next program you
                        start gets; Free, what is left
  System memory         the screen, the module area, the font cache,
  allocation            the system sprites, the RAM disc and so on,
                        and Total: the RAM and the video RAM

The bars get shorter for big amounts, as on RISC OS 3.7: the first
512K is drawn full length, the next 1MB at half, the next 2MB at a
quarter, and so on. So a 24K program still has a bar, and 256MB fits
on the screen. The window is as wide as the longest bar, the Total.

!MemNow, in Diversions, shows the Free figure on the icon bar.


2) Changing the amount of RAM
-----------------------------

  1. Click on the Apps icon on the icon bar, and double-click
     !Configure.
  2. Click on Memory.
  3. The last row, RAM size, has a menu button. Click on it and
     choose 4MB (an A7000), 8MB, 16MB, 32MB, 64MB, 128MB or 256MB.

The change is made at once, and the Task Manager shows the new
figures. It is kept with the rest of the configuration, so the
computer still has that much RAM when you come back (*ResetCMOS sets
it back to 256MB). RISC OS 3.71 had no such setting, of course: it
counted the memory that was fitted. From the command line (F12):

  *Configure RAMSize 64M     the same as choosing 64MB
  *Status RAMSize            shows the setting

With 4MB you have an A7000, which has no video RAM: the screen takes
its memory from the RAM, 768K for a 1024 x 768 desktop in 256
colours, and not much is left. A program that needs more than there
is won't start, and says so:

  "188K free memory is needed before the application will start..."

Quit programs you don't need, make the RAM disc smaller (section 3),
or choose more RAM. !Warning, in Video, warns about playing films on
a machine without video RAM, as it does on a real A7000.


3) Sharing the memory out
-------------------------

In the Task Manager, the red bars can be dragged with SELECT: Next,
Free, Font cache, System sprites and RAM disc (and Screen memory on an
A7000). Making one bigger takes the memory from Free; making it
smaller gives it back. Dragging Free changes Next.

The RAM disc can't be made smaller than the files on it. Drag an empty
RAM disc's bar right down and it goes, icon and all; drag it up again
to get it back.

The other rows in !Configure's Memory window (screen memory, system
heap, module area, font cache, system sprites, RAM disc) are the sizes
the computer starts with: as on RISC OS, they take effect the next
time the desktop starts (reload the page). Setting the RAM disc to 0
starts the desktop without one. The commands are *Configure FontSize,
RAMFSSize, RMASize, ScreenSize, SpriteSize and SystemSize, followed by
a size such as 64K.


4) Limits
---------

These are RISC OS 3.7's:

  A program's slot       at most 28MB (28640K): the most memory a
                         program can have on RISC OS 3.7
  RAM disc               at most 128MB
  Font cache, sprites    at most 16MB each


5) Things to know
-----------------

  - The memory is pretend: the figures agree everywhere (the Task
    Manager, !MemNow, BASIC programs asking the Wimp for memory,
    *Status), but the web browser's own memory doesn't change, and a
    program doesn't run faster with more.
  - A desktop too big for the 2MB of video RAM (bigger than about
    1920 x 1080) takes the rest of its screen memory from the RAM.
  - Making the RAM smaller shrinks the RAM disc, the font cache and
    the system sprites if the programs running would not fit
    otherwise.
